/-!
# The Benedek–Calderón–Panzone theorem

This file was automatically generated by Claude.

This file proves the **Benedek–Calderón–Panzone theorem**: vector-valued Calderón–Zygmund theory
for convolution operators on `ℝⁿ` with operator-valued kernels, and its corollary for
`ℓ²`-valued square functions. The statements of the main results depend on Mathlib only (and on
the local definitions `vczWeakConst`, `vczConst` of the constants); the proofs reuse the dyadic
Calderón–Zygmund selection, Young's inequality, Hölder and the Fatou lemma of
`VariationalSW.Auto.CalderonZygmund`, and the Marcinkiewicz interpolation theorem of
`VariationalSW.Auto.MarcinkiewiczInterpolation`.

## Statement

Let `n ≥ 1`, `E`, `F` complex Banach spaces and `K : ℝⁿ → (E →L[ℂ] F)` Bochner integrable
(a qualitative assumption: no constant depends on `‖K‖₁`). Write
`T f(x) = ∫ K(x - y) f(y) dy` (the Bochner integral, `0` where it diverges; for `f ∈ L^p`,
`1 ≤ p ≤ ∞`, it converges absolutely for a.e. `x`). Assume, for some `A ≥ 0`,
* the `L²` bound `‖T g‖_{L²(F)} ≤ A ‖g‖_{L²(E)}` for all `g ∈ L²(ℝⁿ; E)`;
* Hörmander's condition `∫_{|x| > 2|y|} ‖K(x - y) - K(x)‖ dx ≤ A` for all `y`.
Then, with constants depending only on `n` (and `p`):
* `vcz_weakType_one_one`: `|{‖T f‖ > t}| ≤ C_n A t⁻¹ ‖f‖₁` for `f ∈ L¹`, `t > 0`;
* `vcz_eLpNorm_le`: `‖T f‖_p ≤ C_{n,p} A ‖f‖_p` for `1 < p ≤ 2` and `f ∈ L^p`;
* `vcz_eLpNorm_le_of_innerProductSpace`: if `E`, `F` are Hilbert spaces, the same for all
  `1 < p < ∞`.
The constants are explicit: `C_n = vczWeakConst n = (4√n)^n + 4·2^n + 4`, and
`C_{n,p} = vczConst n p` (`M(1,2,p) C_n^{2/p-1}` for `p < 2` with `M` the Marcinkiewicz
constant, `1` for `p = 2`, and the value at the conjugate exponent for `p > 2`).

The square function corollary (`vcz_eLpNorm_sqFun_le`, `vcz_volume_lt_sqFun_le`) concerns a
finite family `(k_i)_{i ∈ s}` of integrable scalar kernels with
`∑_{i ∈ s} ‖k_i * g‖₂² ≤ A² ‖g‖₂²` and
`∫_{|x| > 2|y|} (∑_{i ∈ s} |k_i(x - y) - k_i(x)|²)^{1/2} dx ≤ A`; it gives
`‖(∑_{i ∈ s} |k_i * f|²)^{1/2}‖_p ≤ C_{n,p} A ‖f‖_p` for `1 < p < ∞` (and the weak type
`(1,1)` bound), with constants independent of `s` and the kernels. It is the case
`E = ℂ`, `F = ℓ²(s) = EuclideanSpace ℂ s` of the Hilbert-space theorem, with the kernel
`vczSqKernel s k x = (z ↦ z (k_i(x))_{i ∈ s})`.

## Proof

* The convolution is Mathlib's `K ⋆[vczPair E F, volume] f` with the evaluation pairing
  `vczPair E F`; `vcz_convolution_eq` identifies it with `∫ K(x - y) f(y) dy`.
* **Calderón–Zygmund decomposition** at height `s = t / A`: the maximal dyadic cubes of the
  scalar development are selected for the scalar function `‖f‖` (`vczNorm f`), so that
  `Ω = czBadSet (vczNorm f) s` has `|Ω| ≤ s⁻¹ ‖f‖₁` and `‖f‖ ≤ s` a.e. off `Ω` (Lebesgue
  differentiation); the good part `vczGood f s` is `f` off `Ω` and the Bochner average
  `f_Q = |Q|⁻¹ ∫_Q f ∈ E` (`vczAvg`) on each selected cube `Q`, so that `‖g‖ ≤ 2^n s`,
  `‖g‖₁ ≤ ‖f‖₁`, and the bad part `b = f - g` has mean zero on each selected cube.
* `T g` is estimated by Chebyshev and the `L²` hypothesis; for `T b`, cancellation
  (`∫_Q K(x - c_Q) b = K(x - c_Q) ∫_Q b = 0`) and the Hörmander condition give
  `∫_{ℝⁿ ∖ Ω*} ‖T b‖ ≤ A ‖b‖₁` (`vcz_lintegral_compl_czStar_le`), and `|Ω*| ≤ (4√n)^n |Ω|`.
  The case `A = 0` follows by letting `A' ↓ 0`.
* For `1 < p < 2`, Marcinkiewicz interpolation (`eLpNorm_le_of_hasWeakTypeOn`) between the weak
  type `(1,1)` and the `L²` bound; `p = 2` is the hypothesis.
* For Hilbert spaces and `p > 2`: the adjoint kernel `vczAdjoint K x = K(-x)†` is integrable,
  satisfies Hörmander's condition with the same `A`, and (by duality and the `L²` hypothesis)
  the `L²` bound with the same `A`; the duality identity `∫ ⟪φ, T f⟫ = ∫ ⟪T* φ, f⟫`
  (`vcz_integral_inner_convolution`, Fubini) and dual testing for Hilbert-space-valued
  functions (`vcz_eLpNorm_le_of_dual_test`) reduce the bound to the case `p' < 2` for `K*`.

## References

* A. Benedek, A.-P. Calderón, R. Panzone, *Convolution operators on Banach space valued
  functions*, Proc. Nat. Acad. Sci. U.S.A. **48** (1962), 356–365.
* L. Grafakos, *Classical Fourier Analysis*, 3rd ed., Graduate Texts in Mathematics 249,
  Springer, 2014, §5.6 (vector-valued inequalities).
-/

